.TH WRIT\-PROVE 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-prove \- print only the statements that show SUBJECT holds TAG
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit prove\fR \fB\-\-as\fR\ \fIID\fR... [\fB\-\-in\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-at\fR\ \fITIME\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR\ \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-\-leaf\-ceiling\fR\ \fIDUR\fR] [\fB\-\-fetch\fR] [\fB\-\-require\-records\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fISUBJECT\fR \fITAG\fR
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Like \fBwrit check\fR, but print a bundle with only the identity logs and statements the answer needs. Give a verifier this proof instead of everything you have. Statements show who you know, so share only what a verifier needs.
.PP
writ reads statements, identity logs and revocations from the \-\-in files, from standard input when it is a file or a pipe, and from each \-\-as identity directory. The order does not matter.
.PP
An identity may publish revocation records (see writ\-init(1)). Then its long\-lived statements count only with a record from it that is younger than its max\-age; a record also lists what it revoked. Records come from the input, from \-\-state, or with \-\-fetch from the URLs in the identity's log. A record is signed, so it does not matter who delivers it.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fISUBJECT\fR
an id, an identity directory, a key file, or an OpenSSH public key
.TP
\fITAG\fR
a list of words from general to specific, such as '(ssh nas)'
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fIID\fR
the identity whose point of view to use: an id or an identity directory
.TP
\fB\-\-in\fR \fIPATH\fR
read statements from PATH; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-at\fR \fITIME\fR
answer for TIME instead of now: 2027\-01\-01, 2027\-01\-01T12:00Z, or @SECONDS
.TP
\fB\-\-state\fR \fIDIR\fR
keep state in DIR between runs: the identity logs, revocations and revocation records seen, and the clock floor
.TP
\fB\-\-leaf\-ceiling\fR \fIDUR\fR
accept grants to leaf keys only if they last at most DUR (at most 24h)
.TP
\fB\-\-fetch\fR
when an answer needs a revocation record that is missing or too old, get it from the URLs its identity publishes, and ask again; only that identity's log and records are taken from what a URL returns. libcurl honours the http_proxy, https_proxy and no_proxy environment variables
.TP
\fB\-\-require\-records\fR
refuse statements by identities that publish no revocation records, except roots given as directories
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print nothing; only set the exit status
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Send only what nas needs to let alice in:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ prove \-\-as me alice '(ssh nas)' < bundle.writ > alice\-nas.writ
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-check\fR(1), \fBwrit\-export\fR(1)
